WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.030
I copied the same five lines into my

2
00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:06.370
program four times, for four classes.

3
00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:09.520
Then I found a bug in them, and

4
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.540
had to fix it four times.

5
00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.810
A function would have fixed it once.

6
00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:18.330
Today: functions, and how to test them.

7
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.680
A function is a named block of

8
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.440
code that does one job.

9
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.180
You define it once with def, its name,

10
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.220
and its inputs, called parameters.

11
00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:32.790
Then you call it by name, as often as

12
00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.570
you like, with different values.

13
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.480
Inside a function, return hands a

14
00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.100
value back to whoever called it.

15
00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.360
print only shows something on the

16
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.300
screen, and the value is lost.

17
00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:48.200
If you want to use the answer, in a sum, a

18
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.900
test or another function, return it.

19
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.960
Variables made inside a function live

20
00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.060
only inside it, while it runs.

21
00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:58.970
That’s called scope.

22
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.200
It means a function can’t accidentally

23
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.780
change your program’s variables, and you

24
00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.200
can understand it on its own.

25
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.220
Now the engineer’s habit:

26
00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.320
test every function.

27
00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.210
An assert line checks that something is

28
00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:17.070
true, and stops with an error if it isn’t.

29
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.460
Write a few asserts with answers

30
00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:20.780
you worked out by hand,

31
00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.720
including the awkward cases.

32
00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:24.720
Quick check!

33
00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:27.990
A function prints its answer

34
00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.030
instead of returning it.

35
00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:34.300
You write total equals add

36
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.220
of three and four.

37
00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.610
What is total?

38
00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:40.120
None.

39
00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:42.510
The seven appears on the screen,

40
00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:44.410
but the function gives back

41
00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:46.810
nothing, which Python calls None.

42
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.200
Use return when the

43
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:50.500
answer is needed later.

44
00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.780
Use print only to show it to a person.

45
00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:57.110
Good functions are small and

46
00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:58.070
named for their job.

47
00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:00.750
Is leap year, given a year.

48
00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:02.990
Grade, given a mark.

49
00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:05.690
Total cost, given a price

50
00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:07.370
and a number of items.

51
00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:10.760
Each one is easy to test on its own.

52
00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:15.220
Testing only easy cases proves little.

53
00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:19.370
Try the edges: an empty list, a single

54
00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:22.210
item, zero, a negative number.

55
00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.200
An average of an empty

56
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.800
list divides by zero.

57
00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:29.510
A good test finds that before a user does.

58
00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:33.090
In the app, open Step through a

59
00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.090
program and choose A function.

60
00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:37.510
Step through and watch the

61
00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:39.970
program jump into area and back.

62
00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.690
Notice that its own variables

63
00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:44.750
exist only while it runs.

64
00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:46.870
Your turn.

65
00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:49.430
You’ve written average of marks.

66
00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.680
Which extra test is most useful?

67
00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:55.070
The empty list.

68
00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.770
Remember: a function is a named job,

69
00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.990
defined once with def and called many

70
00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:02.570
times.

71
00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.460
return hands back a value;

72
00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.500
print only shows it.

73
00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:10.830
Variables inside a function stay inside.

74
00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:14.490
And test with assert, edges included.

75
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.380
Try it yourself: write a function for

76
00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.080
something you do often, like working out

77
00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.100
your average or the cost of a trip.

78
00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.380
Then write three asserts, including one

79
00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:28.640
awkward case, and make them all pass.
